p TypeScript project structure (package.json, tsconfig.json, build config)","status":"completed","priority":"high"},{"content":"Create CSV data loader with normalization for team names, dates, and UTF-8 encoding","status":"completed","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement query engine for matches, teams, players, competitions, and statistics","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement MCP server exposing tools for natural language queries","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write unit and BDD-style tests covering all required query categories","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Build project and run tests, fixing any failures","status":"pending","priority":"high"}],"truncated":false},"title":"4 todos","time":{"start":1782352303409,"end":1782352303412}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me update the loader to deduplicate using the existing matchesSameFixture function or use the key. I'll keep the key approach. But let me fix the bug - parseIntSafe for home_goal might not parse properly when row.home_goal is present but parseIntSafe returns null because parseNumber expects string. Actually in csv parse it's all strings. But for BR-Football-Dataset, the score values are like \"1.0\" and parseIntSafe would parse as 1. Actually parseNumber will return 1, then Math.floor(1)=1. Good.\n\nBut I also noticed in Libertadores, home_goal and away_goal values might be strings like \"2\". parseIntSafe will handle that.\n\nLet me now create the query engine.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_efc79c34d001Cv5YQzO4JOm1rW","sessionID":"ses_1038a1a43ffeeWzYwhyWkjkoD2","messageID":"msg_efc79906b001oxEevZevBl3NIu"}}
